#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# parallel_map

#% text
# `parallel_map(f, xs)` gives the list `[f(x1), f(x2), ...]`, as `map(f, xs)` does, with the calls evaluated on the session's worker threads.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is exactly that of `map(f, xs)`: the same values in the same order.

#% item
# The calls are split at once into runs of about n/(4 threads) elements, whatever each call costs.

#% item
# Printed output and assignments inside the calls happen in the serial order: such calls are evaluated again in order after the parallel part.

#% item
# The worker threads are the session's (`--threads` for `mc-repl`). With one thread the calls run one after another.

#% item
# `map(f, xs, parallel: true)` is the same as `parallel_map(f, xs)`. For anything but a list, `parallel_map` maps as `map` does.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A function applied to every element:

#% cell
parallel_map(f, [a, b, c])
#% output
#= [f(a), f(b), f(c)]

#% cell
parallel_map(k => factorial(k), range(6))
#% output
#= [1, 2, 6, 24, 120, 720]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Over the arguments of another head:

#% cell
parallel_map(f, g(1, 2))
#% output
#= g(f(1), f(2))

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# The option form of `map`:

#% cell
map(k => k^2 + 1, range(5), parallel: true)
#% output
#= [2, 5, 10, 17, 26]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Expensive values, one per element: here a numerical integral for each decay rate.

#% cell
parallel_map(g => n_integrate(exp(-g*t)*cos(t)^2, [t, 0, 20]), [0.5, 1.0, 2.0])
#% output
#= [1.0587878701456346, 0.5999999994140479, 0.37499999999999983]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same result as `map`:

#% cell
parallel_map(k => next_prime(10^k), range(8)) == map(k => next_prime(10^k), range(8))
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `map`, `parallel_table`, `parallel_sum`, `map_thread`
